diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — inter-AS peering congestion causing huge latency between
pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2) — is not supported by any measured
evidence in the live lab. A 10-packet ICMP probe from pc1 to pc2 completed with
0% loss and an average RTT of 1.1 ms, which is normal for a back-to-back
container topology. The eBGP session between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2)
over the 193.10.11.0/24 peering link is fully established (state Established,
uptime 2m11s, zero InQ/OutQ depth). Both routers are correctly advertising and
receiving the expected prefixes: router1 learns 200.1.1.0/24 via BGP and
router2 learns 195.11.14.0/24 via BGP, with matching kernel FIB entries.
Traffic-control (tc) inspection of every interface in the path — router1:eth0,
router2:eth0, router1:eth1, router2:eth1, pc1:eth0, and pc2:eth0 — reveals only
the default fq_codel qdisc with zero drops, zero backlog, and no artificially
injected delay or bandwidth cap. Both routers carry `debug bgp keepalives` and
`debug bgp updates in/out` in their running config, but the FRR log files are
only 4 KB / 41 lines, causing no measurable system stress. The root cause of
the ticket is a false premise: there is no congestion, no added latency, and no
fault anywhere in the topology. No device needs to be restarted; no corrective
action is required.
=== END ===
